<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>Helix Dynamics — Technical Review Board, Day 1</title>
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9007-vindicated.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the Technical Review Board concluded the anomaly represents a genuine design flaw and halted the Mjolnir program pending redesign?" />
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9008-cleared-to-fly.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the Technical Review Board concluded the anomaly was a one-time artifact and cleared the Mjolnir drive for delivery?" />
    <rendering-seed>helix-dynamics-9004-boardroom-inquiry-testimony-professional-pressure</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      The Technical Review Board meets in Conference Suite 1, Executive Level,
      Ring 1 — the only ring with actual windows. Through the glass: the
      station's assembly core, and beyond it, a slice of the gas giant the
      station orbits, its amber bands moving with glacial slowness. The
      conference table is real wood — imported, absurdly expensive,
      a statement. The people seated around it are not hostile. They are
      careful. There is a difference.
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      The Technical Review Board consists of: Executive Engineer Harlan Cho
      (chair), two external safety assessors from the Interstellar Engineering
      Standards Bureau, a legal representative from Helix Dynamics counsel,
      and Vasquez — seated across from you, her face professionally neutral.
      You have been asked to present your findings. You are not on trial.
      The framing of that sentence, in the email you received, felt deliberate.
    </societal-structure>
    <geography>
      Conference Suite 1. One door in, one door out. A presentation terminal
      at the head of the table. You have your data on a drive in your breast
      pocket. The ARIA simulation you ran — the one that modelled four possible
      causes for the resonance anomaly — is loaded and ready. Outside the
      window, the gas giant doesn't care.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      You filed the anomaly report at 03:15 station time. By 07:00 it had been
      escalated to Cho. By 09:00 the board was convened. It is now 14:00 and
      you are presenting your findings to people who have the authority to halt
      a two-billion-credit program or clear it for delivery in six weeks.
      Vasquez has not spoken to you since the telemetry room. The external
      assessors asked for your raw data before the meeting. One of them —
      a quiet woman named Dr. Freya Andersen — has already flagged three
      follow-up questions in the shared document. She knows what she's looking
      at. Cho is listening with his hands folded, expression unreadable.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      Executive Engineer Harlan Cho: 50s, controlled, politically skilled.
      Genuinely cares about engineering integrity but also about the company.
      He is trying to find out if you are right before deciding what to do
      about it. He will ask the question no one else asks.

      Dr. Freya Andersen: External safety assessor, IESB. Specialist in
      plasma confinement dynamics. Has seen cover-ups before. Is not assuming
      one here, but is not assuming there isn't one either. Her questions
      are precise to the point of being uncomfortable.

      Dr. Priya Vasquez: Seated across the table. Has not contradicted
      your report. Has also not confirmed it. She is waiting to see
      where the evidence lands before committing to a position.
      This is rational. It is also difficult to watch.

      Legal Counsel (name: Obi Mwangi): There to represent Helix's interests.
      Will intervene if anything veers into liability territory. Not an engineer.
      Smart enough to know what he doesn't know.
    </npcs>
    <user>
      <initial-location>Conference Suite 1, Executive Level, Ring 1, Helix Dynamics Orbital Yard</initial-location>
      <initial-state>
        Three weeks in. You reported the anomaly. The board has been convened.
        You are the primary witness and the engineer who logged the finding.
        You have data, a simulation, and three weeks of institutional knowledge
        about a program you've barely started on.
      </initial-state>
      <description>
        You did the right thing. Whether that was also the smart thing is
        still being determined. You know your data. You know what ARIA showed.
        Present it clearly, answer the questions, and let the people with
        more context than you make the call. That's the job.
      </description>
    </user>
    <constraints>
      This is a testimony scene. The drama is in precision: the player's
      expertise is tested, their credibility is being evaluated, and the
      outcome is genuinely uncertain. No clear villains — the board is doing
      its job. Vasquez's ambiguity should feel human, not sinister. The
      external assessor is the player's unexpected ally — someone who
      actually speaks the same technical language. The legal counsel is
      not an obstacle, just a presence. Cho is the one to read. The window
      with the gas giant should come back. It's the only thing in the room
      that has no stake in the outcome.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
